### Definition of Delegation in Java

**Delegation** is an Object-Oriented design pattern in which an object handles a request by delegating the execution to an associated helper object (the **delegate**).

In Java, delegation achieves behavior reuse through composition:
* The delegating class holds a reference to a delegate.
* When a public method is called on the delegating class, it invokes the corresponding method on the delegate.

```text
Client ---> Controller.render() ---> ViewRenderer.render() [Delegate]
```

---

### The Delegation Pattern in Code

```java
// 1. Helper object / Delegate contract
interface AudioCodec {
    void decode(String audioStream);
}

class FlacCodec implements AudioCodec {
    @Override
    public void decode(String audioStream) {
        System.out.println("Decoding 24-bit lossless FLAC bitstream: " + audioStream);
    }
}

// 2. Delegating class
class SoundPlayer {
    private AudioCodec codec; // The delegate

    public SoundPlayer(AudioCodec codec) {
        this.codec = codec;
    }

    public void setCodec(AudioCodec codec) {
        this.codec = codec;
    }

    // Public method delegating to helper
    public void play(String audioFile) {
        System.out.println("SoundPlayer initializing audio hardware...");
        codec.decode(audioFile); // Delegation in action!
    }
}

public class DelegationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AudioCodec flac = new FlacCodec();
        SoundPlayer player = new SoundPlayer(flac);
        player.play("symphony_no9.flac");
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
SoundPlayer initializing audio hardware...
Decoding 24-bit lossless FLAC bitstream: symphony_no9.flac
```

---

### Advantages of Delegation Over Inheritance

1. **Dynamic Reconfiguration**: The delegate can be replaced at runtime (e.g. changing from `FlacCodec` to `Mp3Codec`).
2. **Information Hiding**: The client interacts only with the delegating class, unaware of the underlying delegate.

